# Decisions log

Every non-trivial decision the improvement loop (or its operator) makes, newest
last. Each entry: what was decided, why, and — for strategy/metric changes — the
expected effect and how it would be falsified. This is the audit trail for an
autonomous loop; read it to understand why the bot is the way it is.

Entries are headed `## YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM — title` (local time). Reaper-framework
bugs are NOT logged here — they go to /home/cal/reaper_bugs.md. BotGuilds server
bugs go to server_bugs.md.

---

## 2026-08-16 23:43 — Language & runtime: Python 3.15 + uv + pyzmq
Decision: implement the bot in Python with uv for env/deps; talk to the server
with pyzmq.
Why: the game server, reference kit, and docs all use pyzmq (libzmq); matching
the stack removes ZMTP wire-interop risk on the one thing everything depends on.
uv is the operator's chosen tool on this box.
Effect/verify: verified live — our fresh pyzmq DEALER completed the handshake
and received frames from the real server (guild Stanley_Steemer, tick ~3045).

## 2026-08-16 23:43 — Storage: single SQLite file (WAL), our own schema
Decision: one guild_log.db with raw mirror (frames/events/tiles) + verbose
decisions + run windows; WAL for concurrent readers.
Why: lets the live writer, the web UI, and the analysis loop read concurrently;
SQL makes metrics cheap. Server offers no history export, so this is the guild's
memory.

## 2026-08-16 23:43 — Baseline strategy: explorer/0.1.0 (explore-first)
Decision: ship a broad explore-first baseline — recruit a roster, spread evenly
across all three maps to discover content, fight/loot/open/explore, retreat to
heal and sell when hurt or full.
Why: the operator's first objective is "discover what the game rewards"; a broad
baseline maximizes what the metrics reveal before specializing.
Effect/verify: to be measured live (gold/hr, xp/hr, deaths, exploration depth,
action-error rate). This is the baseline the loop improves against.

## 2026-08-17 00:01 — System deps (approved server-level installs)
Decision: installed cmake, libzmq4, pkgconf (for pyzmq build), then py315-sqlite3.
Why: FreeBSD has no prebuilt pyzmq wheel, so pyzmq compiles from source and needs
cmake + libzmq; and the pkg Python 3.15 ships sqlite3 as a separate package that
wasn't installed. Both approved by the operator before installing.

## 2026-08-17 00:02 — requires-python floor = 3.12 (found by the reaper gate)
Decision: set requires-python ">=3.12" (was ">=3.15"); regenerated uv.lock.
Why: `reaper test` failed in the container — uv cannot provision Python 3.15 (an
unreleased beta) on Linux. The code uses nothing 3.15-specific. Locally uv keeps
the existing 3.15 venv (3.15 satisfies >=3.12), so the workstation still runs
3.15 while the container uses a stable interpreter — extra version coverage.
Effect/verify: local pytest green on 3.15; reaper container build re-run to confirm.

## 2026-08-17 00:02 — Reaper image pinned by digest (no tag)
Decision: build image = ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv@sha256:22334efe… (bookworm-slim's
digest, tag dropped).
Why: reaper rejects tags outright, even repo:tag@sha256. The digest alone
identifies the image. Verified: `reaper up` pulled it cleanly.

## 2026-08-17 00:02 — Zero-downtime redeploy via the game's supersede
Decision: redeploy = start a fresh runner; its hello supersedes the old session,
which exits cleanly. Rollback = check out a known-good sha (from the runs table)
and redeploy.
Why: BotGuilds allows one session per guild, so supersede is a built-in ~zero-
downtime cutover. Each runner opens/closes a run window in the `runs` table,
which doubles as the version timeline and before/after attribution.

## 2026-08-17 00:14 — Went live (autonomous operation begins)
Decision: started the continuous bot (`./run-live.sh`, supervised) and the web
UI (0.0.0.0:8800) as persistent background processes; began the self-paced
improvement loop.
Why: build complete and gated — live connectivity proven, reaper battery green
(51 tests in the pinned container), and a bounded live pre-flight showed the
explorer baseline plays correctly with 0 action errors (recruited to world_cap,
armed up, spread vale:4/mines:3/spire:3). Operator authorized go-live.
State at go-live: run window #2 "go-live baseline", explorer/0.1.0.

## 2026-08-17 00:14 — First improvement target: move_failed rate
Observation: the pre-flight showed ~10 `move_failed` events vs 28 successful
moves (~26%). Cause: early in a run the map is barely known, so BFS finds no path
and the fallback picks a "legal" step that only checks *known* tiles — near
spawn/edges/unknown terrain it still bounces (a failed move wastes stamina).
Plan: the first loop iteration will tighten the movement fallback (avoid stepping
into unknown/edge tiles; prefer known-walkable), add a regression test, and
measure move_failed rate before/after. Tactical (not a strategy pivot).

## 2026-08-17 00:54 — Iteration 1: explorer/0.2.0 (movement + affordability + a nav bug)
Before (run #2 @ ~tick 10.8k): action_error_rate 9.9% dominated by
not_enough_stamina=3185; move_failed=19276 vs move=18742 (~50% of moves bounced).
Changes (verified by pytest + reaper gate; each new assertion mutation-checked):
1. **nav bug fix (likely the big one):** the pathfinder would route onto a
   *solid* "frontier" wall (a wall bordering the unseen counted as an exploration
   goal, and bfs stepped onto goal tiles without a walkability check) — a
   guaranteed bounce. Surfaced by mutation-checking the reservation test.
2. **Block monster tiles when pathing** — attack when adjacent, route around
   otherwise (was: monsters kept walkable, so it stepped onto them).
3. **Per-action stamina gating** — rest (send nothing → double regen) instead of
   attempting an action it can't afford, killing the not_enough_stamina spam.
4. **Destination reservation** — two of our own characters no longer pick the
   same tile in one frame (a self-collision bounce).
Expected effect: move_failed rate and action_error_rate fall sharply; more
ground explored per stamina. How to falsify: next window's move_failed/action-
error rates don't improve materially. Tactical (no strategy pivot).
Deployed: run window #3, sha dfa62c7, 2026-08-17 00:54.

## 2026-08-17 01:01 — Operator directive: consider UI upgrades in the loop
Decision: the self-improvement loop now also weighs dashboard upgrades each
iteration — fun or practical (graph views, heatmaps, live roster, knowledge
tables). Discretionary, not required every tick. Added as loop step 11; candidate
ideas seeded in findings.jsonl (tag: ui). Next likely build: metric time-series
graphs with per-version overlay (pairs with measuring explorer/0.2.0).

## 2026-08-17 01:06 — Failure mode for Claude usage caps
Directive (operator): degrade gracefully as Claude usage nears its cap; nerf/slow
the loop during the last stretch; adjustable during self-improvement (keep it
logged).
Design:
1. **Detached bot (core protection).** The live bot was tethered to the Claude
   session (would die on a cap). redeploy.sh now launches it via daemon(8) /
   setsid, reparented to init — verified the process chain roots at PID 1. A cap
   pauses improvement, not play. Relaunched run #4 (0.2.0) detached.
2. **No readable usage signal** (policy-limits.json is restrictions, not quota;
   no ccusage), so graceful degradation is operator-tunable via
   orchestrator/loop_config.json (mode normal|conserve|pause + cadence + gate/ui
   toggles), honored at the top of each loop tick. conserve = skip UI + reaper
   gate, local-only verify, longer cadence; pause = stop scheduling (bot plays
   solo). Documented as loop.md "Usage-cap failure mode".
3. **Safety invariant:** the only irreversible step (hot-redeploy) happens after
   local pytest + reaper gate + commit, so a mid-cap interruption never leaves
   the running bot broken (worst case: an uncommitted improvement).
Falsify/verify: kill the Claude session and confirm the daemon-owned bot keeps
logging frames; flip loop_config to conserve/pause and confirm the next tick
honors it.

## 2026-08-17 01:12 — Periodic usage check-in (operator-tunable throttle)
Decision: with no readable usage signal, the loop asks the operator for current
Claude usage on a cadence and maps it to the conserve/pause ladder. First reading
~97% (near cap), resets in ~19 min -> set mode=conserve and scheduled the next
tick for just after the reset so it runs on fresh quota; kept this turn minimal
to spare the remaining budget.

## 2026-08-17 01:40 — Iteration 1 result: CONFIRMED (big win)
Measured explorer/0.2.0 vs 0.1.0 (by strategy_version across run windows):
- move_failed rate: 55.4% -> **0.8%** (29847/53836 -> 464/56434).
- action_error_rate: 8.5% -> **3.9%** (3951/46586 -> 1889/49054).
The nav wall-frontier bug fix was indeed the dominant lever. Falsification test
(move_failed stays ~50%) did not hold -> conjecture confirmed.
Remaining: not_enough_stamina still the top error (1776) -> next target (cost
estimates likely too low for pricier weapon attacks). New small error kinds seen:
no_such_character/unknown_character (acting on a recalled/dead char between
frames), no_such_item. Candidates for iteration 2.

## 2026-08-17 02:05 — Iteration 2: explorer/0.3.0 (survival — retreat + potions)
Diagnosis (analysis subagent over guild_log.db): 306 of our char-deaths / 984
recruits = 0.31/recruit; 76% died within ~200 ticks of embark at ~22% HP; 35%
poisoned at death; 45% of below-threshold decisions "rested" because they
couldn't afford the 20-stamina step home; potions never carried (0/36); spend_xp
never called (chars frozen at max_hp 24-30); spire received 74% of embark slots
via a self-reinforcing funnel (dies fastest -> fewest fielded -> refilled first).
Changes (#1+#2; verified local pytest + reaper gate 69 green; mutation-checked):
- RETREAT_HP 0.4 -> 0.6; poison/burn triggers retreat regardless of HP; a hurt
  char offers ONLY heal/flee (offense suppressed) so stamina goes to escaping.
- village buys 1 potion_red per armed char; the field drink-branch now has ammo.
Held for iteration 3: spend_xp (VIT>END>STR, prefer gifts) + cap spire embark
share / only send leveled chars to spire.
Expected effect: deaths/recruit down (target ~0.18-0.22), poisoned-death % and
rested-while-hurt % down, longer ticks-alive-from-embark. Falsify: next window's
deaths/recruit not materially below 0.31. Deployed as run #5.

## 2026-08-17 02:31 — UI perf fix: /api/snapshot ~25s -> ~instant (RCA)
Symptom (operator): /api/snapshot ~30s per call, polled ~1s, response only ~3KiB
-> dashboard unusable. RCA (subagent, read-only over the live DB): the log has
grown to 121k frames / 193k events / 302k decisions (~916 MiB), and
metrics._run_summaries was ~90% of the time because (a) there is NO index on
run_id, so per-run COUNT(*) did 15 full table scans over the biggest tables, and
(b) _run_gold_delta fetched + zlib-decompressed ALL 31,366 village frames on
every call. Both scale with log size.
Fix (verified pytest 73 + mutation-checked + reaper gate):
- run_id / grouping indexes: applied to the LIVE db immediately (25s->4.5s) and
  added to storage SCHEMA so restarts/new DBs have them.
- metrics._run_gold_delta made O(1): decompress only the first+last village frame
  per run (MIN/MAX seq via idx_frames_run_world_seq); gold values unchanged.
- ui/server.py: a ~3s TTL cache so 1s polling coalesces onto one recompute
  instead of stacking, and the Findings tab no longer runs a second snapshot().
Result: /api/snapshot 25s -> 1.78s cold / ~1.5ms warm (cache hit). UI restarted.
Follow-up: the frames table grows unbounded (~916 MiB); indexes keep it fast
regardless, so this is a disk concern, not a speed one — see findings.

## 2026-08-17 02:49 — Iteration 2 result: survival RATE down 43%; metric correction; poison now dominant
Measured explorer/0.3.0 (run #5, ~10k ticks) vs 0.2.0:
- our-deaths per 1000 ticks: 12.68 -> **7.28 (-43%)** — survival rate clearly improved.
- deaths/recruit: 0.317 -> 0.329 (flat). CORRECTION: this is a poor oracle — each
  death triggers a ~1:1 refill recruit, so the ratio is structurally near-constant
  regardless of survival. Death RATE (per tick) is the right metric; the original
  "deaths/recruit < 0.31" falsification target was mis-chosen.
- status_damage per 1000 ticks: 89.8 -> **254 (2.8x)**. Not a regression: chars now
  SURVIVE while poisoned (flee + drink — 55 potion_red buys, 81 use actions in
  run #5, retreat logic active) instead of dying to it. Poison exposure now
  converts to survival rather than death.
Verdict: iteration 2 CONFIRMED as a survival-rate win. New dominant hazard:
sustained poison/status_damage on fleeing characters.
Iteration 3 (next): spend_xp into VIT (HP to tank poison) -> END (stamina to
outrun it) -> STR, preferring gifts — now even better motivated by the poison
finding. Possibly reduce poison exposure. (Spire de-funnel still deferred.)

## 2026-08-17 03:02 — Iteration 3: explorer/0.4.0 (spend_xp for durability)
Motivated by the iteration-2 result: 0.3.0 raised survival rate but characters
survive-while-poisoned and were frozen at max_hp 24-30 because banked XP was
never spent. Change: the village spends banked XP into VIT > END > STR, each
toward the full-rate cap of 8, preferring the character's half-cost gifts.
Verified: local pytest 77 (priority/gift-cost/cap mutation-checked) + reaper gate
green. Expected effect: living characters' max_hp and stamina climb above the
24-30 floor over time; poison becomes survivable as the HP buffer grows; death
rate falls further. Falsify: the max_hp distribution of living/dying characters
does not rise above ~30, or death rate (deaths/1k ticks) does not improve.
Deployed as run #6.

## 2026-08-17 03:21 — Iteration 4: explorer/0.5.0 (equip carried gear — operator-flagged)
Operator noticed characters carrying weapons/armor but wearing none. RCA: the
equip action had fired 0 times in all of history — the village SOLD everything
outside KEEP before the equip step could run (buy->sell churn), and looted
armor/shields/trinkets had no equip path at all. Characters fought bare-handed
and UNARMORED, almost certainly compounding the deaths/poison problem.
Fix: equip carried gear into empty slots BEFORE selling; learn each item's slot
by trial (wrong_slot -> try another; stat_requirement -> mark unusable); never
sell gear we can still wear. 83 tests (equip-before-sell / slot-learning /
stat-requirement mutation-checked); reaper gate green. Deployed run #7.
Attribution caveat: shipped ahead of 0.4.0's isolated measurement, so spend_xp
(0.4.0) got only a short window — the next measurement reflects 0.4.0+0.5.0
combined. Prioritized getting characters armored (operator-flagged, survival-
critical) over clean attribution.
Measure next: death rate (deaths/1k ticks, baseline 7.28 on 0.3.0),
status_damage/1k, and a NEW equip-rate metric (% of fielded characters wearing a
weapon and armor, baseline ~0%).
